The later file-I/O and file-system lectures do not require extra allocator
features. gettimeofday and random allocation choices come from Project I.
rand and srand provide those random choices; gettimeofday provides timing.

DESIGN
The heap is the required static union containing a double and a 4096-byte
character array. The double keeps the beginning aligned to eight bytes.
One static int records whether initialization has happened.

Each chunk begins with a struct header containing two int fields:
    size: total bytes in this chunk, including its header
    allocated: zero for free, one for in use
On the target iLab system, this header uses eight bytes. The header length
is rounded to a multiple of eight. The next chunk starts at offset + size.
No next or previous pointer is needed because all chunks are contiguous.

read_header and write_header use memcpy to copy between a local struct and
the byte array. These local copies exist only while a function runs. All
persistent allocator metadata stays inside the heap.

Initialization creates one free chunk spanning the heap and registers the
leak detector with atexit. Both allocator functions initialize automatically.
malloc rounds data size up to eight bytes and scans for the first free chunk
large enough. It splits only if the remainder can hold a header plus eight
data bytes. Otherwise it gives the caller the entire chunk.

free scans the actual chunk headers for a matching payload start. It does
not read through the caller's pointer. It marks a valid object free, then
merges adjacent free chunks. It reports an invalid pointer and exits with
status 2. Live objects stay in place, and their data is not used as metadata.

The leak detector counts live objects and their payload sizes, including
padding but excluding headers. It prints only when objects remain allocated.
It does not call exit.

Chosen behavior for unspecified cases: free(NULL) does nothing; malloc(0)
reports allocation failure and returns NULL. Oversized requests are rejected
before rounding, avoiding overflow. Ordinary use-after-free cannot be detected
without an allocator call. A stale pointer to an address that has since been
allocated again cannot be distinguished from the new pointer.

MEMLENGTH may be changed or set with -DMEMLENGTH. Use a multiple of eight
that is large enough for a header and payload and fits in int. The supplied
boundary tests assume 4096 bytes and an eight-byte header.

MEMGRIND: FIVE TASKS, 50 COMPLETE RUNS
1. Allocate 8,16,32,64,128,512,1024 bytes; free in reverse order.
2. Allocate 120 one-byte objects; free in allocation order.
3. Randomly allocate a one-byte object or free a random live object until
   120 total allocations have occurred. Then free all remaining objects.
4. Allocate 40 mixed-size objects, free alternating objects, refill those
   holes, free odd-indexed objects, then free even-indexed objects backwards.
5. Allocate 30 objects of 64 bytes. Free adjacent pairs in groups of three,
   replace those pairs with 128-byte objects, then free in reverse groups.
Every task frees all its objects. gettimeofday measures the full workload
repeated 50 times; the program reports elapsed microseconds divided by 50.
Randomness is seeded once with 214 to make runs repeatable.

TEST PLAN: REQUIREMENT -> CHECK -> PROGRAM
Aligned allocation -> check relative payload offsets are multiples of eight
and read/write a double -> test_correctness. The union aligns the heap start.
Non-overlap and preservation -> fill objects with distinct byte patterns,
then check them after allocations and frees -> test_correctness and memtest.
Reuse -> fill the heap, free one hole, and allocate the same size in that
only available hole -> test_correctness.
Coalescing -> fill the heap, free adjacent blocks, and request their combined
space while checking a live guard object's data -> test_correctness.
Exact fits and small remainders -> request 4088 and 4080 data bytes, free,
and confirm the whole heap can be allocated again -> test_correctness.
Repeated operations -> perform 3000 random operations on 32 slots while
checking all live data, then reclaim the whole heap -> test_correctness.
Invalid frees, allocation failures, and leaks -> independent test_errors
executions with the expected diagnostics and exit statuses below.
Stress -> memgrind completes with timing output and no errors or leaks.

TEST PROGRAMS
test_correctness takes no arguments. It uses if statements to check results,
exits with status 1 on a failed check, and prints success when all pass.
memtest takes no arguments. It fills 64 objects with distinct bytes and should
print '0 incorrect bytes'. It returns failure if a byte is incorrect.
Optional memtest builds: -DREALMALLOC uses the standard allocator;
-DLEAK deliberately leaves the objects allocated for leak detection.

test_errors takes exactly one argument. Run each case separately because
invalid free calls must terminate the process:
    ./test_errors outside        expected status 2, bad-pointer diagnostic
    ./test_errors interior       expected status 2, bad-pointer diagnostic
    ./test_errors double         expected status 2, bad-pointer diagnostic
    ./test_errors merged-double  expected status 2, bad-pointer diagnostic
    ./test_errors oom            expected status 0, allocation of 1 fails
    ./test_errors oversize       expected status 0, allocation of 4089 fails
    ./test_errors overflow       expected status 0, largest size_t fails
    ./test_errors zero           expected status 0, allocation of 0 fails
    ./test_errors leak           expected status 0, 32 bytes in 2 leaked objects
    ./test_errors no-leak        expected status 0, no diagnostic
    ./test_errors null           expected status 0, no diagnostic
The interior case also reports its still-live 16-byte object at exit.
Immediately after a command, use echo $? to view its exit status.
To inspect diagnostics separately, use the redirection from the notes:
    ./test_errors outside > output.txt 2> errors.txt
Unknown or missing arguments return status 1.

VALIDATION
All four programs built with local MinGW GCC in C99 mode. All 14 normal,
error, allocation-failure, and leak scenarios passed local execution, with
their output and exit codes checked. This is not an iLab validation; run
make check and the individual error cases on iLab before submission.

